{
  "_comment": "Seed crawl list. `phase` drives crawl order; `platform_guess` is a hint for the ingestion worker, which detects the real one. Domains need a verification pass before the first crawl — the worker's tier-detection will surface bad ones.",
  "_phases": {
    "1": "Direct-from-brand. Mostly Shopify, clean structured data, coherent house aesthetic, natural-fiber-forward. Start here.",
    "2": "Chains with a strong house style. Coverage + recognizability. Boutique score will rank them low, which is correct.",
    "3": "Aggregators and fast fashion. Different ingestion problem (huge catalogs, anti-bot). Defer."
  },
  "_selection_criteria": [
    "Fashionable — a coherent, identifiable house aesthetic rather than trend-chasing.",
    "Good fabrics — natural fibers and real construction, not 100% polyester at a markup.",
    "Crawlable — structured product data (Shopify /products.json or schema.org JSON-LD).",
    "Boutique-leaning — few locations, independent or founder-led where possible."
  ],
  "_fabric_gate": {
    "why": "'Good fabrics' is the criterion that separates a taste index from a catalog, and unlike aesthetic judgment it is machine-checkable — fiber composition sits in the Shopify payload or product copy for most brands.",
    "how": "During ingestion, parse fiber composition per item and compute a natural-fiber ratio (cotton, linen, wool, cashmere, silk, leather, TENCEL/lyocell, viscose-from-certified-source). Roll up to a brand-level median.",
    "gate": "Brands whose median natural-fiber ratio falls below ~50% get flagged for review before entering the index, regardless of how fashionable they look.",
    "note": "This turns a taste criterion into an automated index gate. It is also a strong input to the Boutique Score in Feature 3 — cheap synthetics correlate hard with the chains we want to rank down. Expect it to flag several phase-2 and every phase-3 entry, which is the point."
  },

